---
import LegalPage from '../components/site/LegalPage.astro';
import { legal, site } from '../lib/content';

const toc = [
	{ id: 'summary', label: 'Summary' },
	{ id: 'scope', label: 'Who we are and scope' },
	{ id: 'local-files', label: 'Your local files' },
	{ id: 'cloud-storage', label: 'Cloud storage connections' },
	{ id: 'google', label: 'Google user data' },
	{ id: 'credentials', label: 'Credentials and tokens' },
	{ id: 'crash-reports', label: 'Crash reports' },
	{ id: 'updates', label: 'Software updates' },
	{ id: 'website', label: 'This website' },
	{ id: 'sharing', label: 'Sharing and selling' },
	{ id: 'retention', label: 'Retention and deletion' },
	{ id: 'security', label: 'Security' },
	{ id: 'rights', label: 'Your rights and choices' },
	{ id: 'children', label: 'Children' },
	{ id: 'changes', label: 'Changes to this policy' },
	{ id: 'contact', label: 'Contact' },
];
---

<LegalPage
	title="Privacy Policy"
	eyebrow="Legal"
	description="How Lite Explorer handles your files, your cloud storage accounts, and the little data it sends back to us. Short version: your files stay yours."
	toc={toc}
>
	<section id="summary">
		<h2>Summary</h2>
		<div class="callout">
			<ul>
				<li>
					{site.name} is a desktop app. It runs on your computer and works with your files there.
				</li>
				<li>
					We do <strong>not</strong> run a server that receives your files, file names, or folder
					contents. We do not have user accounts.
				</li>
				<li>
					When you connect Google Drive, Amazon S3, Cloudflare R2, Azure Blob Storage or another
					S3-compatible service, the app talks <strong>directly</strong> from your computer to that
					provider. Your data never passes through Litepod Studio.
				</li>
				<li>
					Passwords, access keys and sign-in tokens are stored in your operating system's secure
					credential store (Keychain, Credential Manager or Secret Service).
				</li>
				<li>
					The app can send anonymous crash and error reports so we can fix bugs. They never
					include file contents.
				</li>
				<li>We never sell your data, and we never use it for advertising.</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section id="scope">
		<h2>Who we are and scope</h2>
		<p>
			{site.name} is developed and published by <strong>{legal.publisher}</strong> ("we", "us",
			"our"). This Privacy Policy explains what information the {site.name} desktop application
			for macOS, Windows and Linux (the "App") and the website at
			{' '}<a href="https://explorer.litepod.sh">explorer.litepod.sh</a> (the "Website") collect, how we
			use it, and the choices you have.
		</p>
		<p>
			The App is open-source software. You can review exactly what it does in its
			{' '}<a href={site.repo} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">public source code</a>.
		</p>
		<p>
			This policy does not cover third-party services that you choose to connect to the App, such
			as Google Drive or Amazon S3. Those services are governed by their own privacy policies.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="local-files">
		<h2>Your local files</h2>
		<p>
			The App reads the folders you open so it can list, preview, search, copy, move, rename,
			compress, extract and delete files on your behalf. All of this happens on your device.
		</p>
		<p>
			To remember your preferences, the App stores a small amount of data in its local
			application-data folder on your computer, for example:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>Settings such as theme, layout, sidebar width and keyboard mode.</li>
			<li>Your favorites, recent locations and open tabs.</li>
			<li>
				The list of cloud storage locations you added (name, provider, bucket or container, region
				or endpoint, and folder prefix). Secrets are not stored here; see
				{' '}<a href="#credentials">Credentials and tokens</a>.
			</li>
			<li>A random installation identifier, used only for crash reports.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			This data stays on your device. We cannot see it. Uninstalling the App and removing its
			application-data folder deletes it.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="cloud-storage">
		<h2>Cloud storage connections</h2>
		<p>
			You can add remote locations from supported providers: Amazon S3, Cloudflare R2, Azure Blob
			Storage, other S3-compatible services, and Google Drive. When you browse or transfer files in
			one of these locations:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				The App sends requests directly from your computer to the provider's API, using the
				credentials you supplied.
			</li>
			<li>
				File names, metadata and file contents move only between your computer and your provider.
				Litepod Studio does not operate any proxy or intermediate server, and we never receive
				this data.
			</li>
			<li>
				The App only performs the actions you request: listing folders, previewing, downloading,
				uploading, renaming, moving and deleting.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Your use of each provider is subject to that provider's own terms and privacy policy.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="google">
		<h2>Google user data</h2>
		<p>
			This section describes how the App accesses, uses, stores and shares data from your Google
			account when you choose to connect Google Drive.
		</p>

		<h3>What we access</h3>
		<p>When you select "Connect Google Drive", the App asks Google for this permission:</p>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table>
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th>Scope</th>
						<th>Why the App needs it</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td><code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive</code></td>
						<td>
							Lite Explorer is a file manager. It shows your whole Drive as a folder tree, so you
							can browse, preview, download, upload, rename, move and delete your own files and
							folders, the same way you manage files on your computer.
						</td>
					</tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p>
			With this permission the App can read the names, folder structure, metadata and contents of
			files in your Google Drive, and create, change or delete them when you ask it to. The App does
			not request access to your Gmail, contacts, calendar or any other Google service.
		</p>

		<h3>How we use it</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Google Drive data is used <strong>only</strong> to provide the file-management features you
				see in the App, and only in response to your actions.
			</li>
			<li>
				The App does not use Google user data for advertising, profiling, analytics, or to train
				artificial intelligence or machine learning models.
			</li>
			<li>
				Nobody at Litepod Studio reads your Google Drive data. The data never reaches our servers.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h3>How we store it</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Sign-in uses Google's OAuth 2.0 flow with PKCE, in your web browser. The App never sees
				your Google password.
			</li>
			<li>
				After you sign in, Google returns a refresh token. The App stores it only in your operating
				system's secure credential store on your device.
			</li>
			<li>
				File contents are downloaded to your computer only when you open, preview or copy them, and
				are saved only where you choose.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h3>How we share it</h3>
		<p>
			We do not share, transfer, sell or disclose Google user data to anyone. Because the App talks
			directly to Google's API from your computer, there is no Litepod Studio server in between.
		</p>

		<h3>Limited Use</h3>
		<p>
			{site.name}'s use and transfer to any other app of information received from Google APIs will
			adhere to the
			{' '}<a
				href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
				target="_blank"
				rel="noreferrer">Google API Services User Data Policy</a
			>, including the Limited Use requirements.
		</p>

		<h3>Removing access</h3>
		<p>You can disconnect Google Drive at any time:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Remove the Google Drive location in the App. This deletes the stored token from your
				device.
			</li>
			<li>
				Revoke the App's access from your Google account at
				{' '}<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"
					>myaccount.google.com/permissions</a
				>. The token then stops working immediately.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section id="credentials">
		<h2>Credentials and tokens</h2>
		<p>
			Access keys, secret keys, account keys and OAuth refresh tokens are saved in the secure
			credential store of your operating system:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>macOS:</strong> Keychain.</li>
			<li><strong>Windows:</strong> Credential Manager.</li>
			<li><strong>Linux:</strong> Secret Service (for example GNOME Keyring or KWallet).</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Credentials never leave your device except when they are sent to the matching provider to
			authenticate your requests. They are never included in crash reports and never sent to
			Litepod Studio. Removing a location in the App deletes its credentials.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="crash-reports">
		<h2>Crash reports</h2>
		<p>
			To find and fix bugs on each operating system, the App can send crash and error reports to
			our error-monitoring service, powered by Sentry. A report can include:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>The App version, operating system, OS version and CPU architecture.</li>
			<li>
				Technical details of the error: the error message, stack trace, and a short trail of the
				App's recent internal events.
			</li>
			<li>Basic performance timings for internal operations.</li>
			<li>
				A random installation identifier, created on first launch. It is not linked to your name,
				email, or any account, and it only lets us count how many installations a problem
				affects.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Reports never include file contents, credentials or tokens. An error message can
			occasionally contain a file or folder path, for example when a file cannot be opened. We use
			this only to diagnose the bug.
		</p>
		<p>
			On first launch the App explains crash reporting. While the App is in its pre-1.0 beta
			period, crash reporting stays on so we can stabilize it. From version 1.0 onward you can
			turn it off at any time in <strong>Settings</strong>. We keep crash reports for a limited
			time (normally up to 90 days) and then delete them.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="updates">
		<h2>Software updates</h2>
		<p>
			The App checks for new versions by downloading a small release manifest from GitHub, where
			our releases are published. Like any web request, GitHub receives your IP address and basic
			request information, under
			{' '}<a
				href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement"
				target="_blank"
				rel="noreferrer">GitHub's privacy statement</a
			>. Updates are cryptographically signed, and the App verifies each signature before
			installing.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="website">
		<h2>This website</h2>
		<p>
			The Website is hosted on Litepod. To understand how visitors use it, we use a self-hosted
			instance of Umami, a privacy-focused analytics tool. Umami:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>Does not use cookies and does not track you across other websites.</li>
			<li>
				Collects aggregate information such as pages visited, referrer, browser, device type,
				country and page performance.
			</li>
			<li>
				May record anonymized interaction sessions (clicks and scrolling on this site) to help us
				improve the layout.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			The Website stores your theme preference (light or dark) in your browser's local storage.
			We do not use advertising or tracking cookies.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="sharing">
		<h2>Sharing and selling</h2>
		<p>We do not sell, rent or trade personal information. We share limited data only with:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Service providers</strong> that run infrastructure for us: our error-monitoring
				service (crash reports) and our hosting provider (the Website). They process data only
				on our behalf.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Authorities</strong>, when the law requires it, to respond to valid legal
				process or to protect the rights and safety of our users or others.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>Google user data is never shared with anyone, as described in <a href="#google">Google user data</a>.</p>
	</section>

	<section id="retention">
		<h2>Retention and deletion</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Local data and credentials</strong> stay on your device until you remove the
				location, delete the App's data folder, or uninstall the App.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Crash reports</strong> are kept for a limited time, normally up to 90 days, and
				then deleted.
			</li>
			<li><strong>Website analytics</strong> are kept in aggregate form.</li>
			<li>We keep no Google Drive data or other cloud storage data, because we never receive it.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			To ask us to delete crash reports linked to your installation, email us and include your
			installation identifier if you can.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="security">
		<h2>Security</h2>
		<p>
			We design the App so that as little data as possible leaves your device. Connections to
			cloud providers and to our crash-reporting service use HTTPS/TLS. Secrets live in the OS
			credential store, not in plain-text files. Updates are signed. No method of storage or
			transmission is completely secure, but we work to protect your information and to fix
			security issues quickly.
		</p>
		<p>
			If you find a security vulnerability, please report it privately to
			{' '}<a href={`mailto:${legal.email}`}>{legal.email}</a> before disclosing it publicly.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="rights">
		<h2>Your rights and choices</h2>
		<p>
			Depending on where you live (for example under the GDPR in the European Union, or under
			privacy laws in the United States, Colombia and elsewhere), you may have the right to
			access, correct, delete, restrict or object to the processing of your personal information,
			and to data portability. You can:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>Remove any cloud location and its credentials from the App.</li>
			<li>Revoke Google Drive access from your Google account settings.</li>
			<li>Turn off crash reporting in Settings (from version 1.0).</li>
			<li>Block Website analytics with your browser or a content blocker.</li>
			<li>Contact us to exercise any of your rights.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			We will not discriminate against you for exercising these rights. You may also file a
			complaint with your local data-protection authority.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="children">
		<h2>Children</h2>
		<p>
			The App and the Website are not directed at children under 13 (or the minimum age in your
			country). We do not knowingly collect personal information from children.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="changes">
		<h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
		<p>
			We may update this policy when the App changes. We will change the effective date at the top
			of this page. If a change is significant, we will also announce it in the App's release
			notes. If you continue to use the App after a change, the updated policy applies.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="contact">
		<h2>Contact</h2>
		<p>
			For questions about this policy or your data, contact {legal.publisher} at
			{' '}<a href={`mailto:${legal.email}`}>{legal.email}</a>.
		</p>
		<p>
			See also our <a href={legal.termsPath}>Terms of Service</a>.
		</p>
	</section>
</LegalPage>
